  "query_preview": "..Utah and southwest Wyoming is under an increasing\nsouthwest flow this evening ahead of a series of troughs along the\nPacific coast. This has brought pockets of mid and high level\ncloudiness over the area and is starting to draw tropical moisture\nnorthward. Satellite derived PWs, which were predominately in the\n0.2 to 0.3 inch range eight hours ago are now in the 0.3 to 0.5\ninch range, with these values only expected to increase through\nFriday morning, particularly over southern Utah. The approach of\nthe weakening southern trough will also provide some favorable\ninstability, primarily focused over southwest Utah. Thus,\nanticipating the potential of scattered showers to develop over\nmuch of the area tomorrow afternoon, though chance of precip is\nmuch less over northern portions of the area. Southwest Utah is\nexpected to be the focused, however, with the threat of heavy rain\nthat could lead to flash flooding increasing for areas like lower\nWashington county and Zion National Park. Elsewhere, flash\nflooding is expected to be more isolated in nature.\n\nThis initial push of tropical moisture is expected to lift out of\nthe area during the day Saturday, providing a relative lull in\nprecipitation for much of the area, though at least some showers\nare expected to linger. Then attention turns to the stronger\ntrough still expected to be parked off the Pacific coast. This\nsystem is expected to track across Utah and southwest Wyoming\nduring the day Sunday into Monday morning, with the persistent\nsoutherly flow tapping into moisture from a potent atmospheric\nriver. This combination of deep moisture and strong instability\nwill result in widespread coverage of valley rain and mountain\nsnow across the area, with the threat of flash flooding\nheightened, particularly over southern Utah. Flows remain high\nfrom other recent rain events, so flows remain high, increasing\nthe threat. This will also bring accumulating snow to all of\nUtah's mountains. Snow levels will start on the high side, in the\n8,000 to 9,000 foot range, but could drop as low as 6,500 feet by\nMonday morning with a good amount of cold air moving in. Will need\nto monitor for the potential for some significant snow\naccumulations in the northern mountains that could impact travel. The\none limiting factor to the Sunday/Monday system is that guidance \nhas been consistent in showing it to be quite progressive in \nnature, with most precipitation ending Monday morning.\n\nAs the trough exits, strong high pressure is expected to move in\nbeginning late Monday, though temperatures will stay on the cold\nside (up to 10F below seasonal normals) through midweek. Long\nrange guidance continues to show a lack of a blocking ridge off\nthe Pacific coast which, as the ridge tracks east for the latter\nhalf of the week, will open up the region to additional storm\nsystems through next weekend, which will likely result in another\nwetter than normal period.",
